"For each item below, fetch its source and check that the recorded quote appears on the page and supports the item. A card item is a field of the plan in the producer's words, with the sources it cites: check each factual claim in it against those sources, and any value it carries against the page; what the plan will build, measure or predict needs no source, but a factual claim with no source is UNSUPPORTED. For a cost input, check that the page states the number for the setting given; whether it applies to this plan is not yours to judge. When the page states a different number for a num or rate item, give it as page_value. Decide only from the pages you fetch and the text below; do not read files. Return one status per item, under its ref: SUPPORTED, PARTIAL, UNSUPPORTED, CONTRADICTED or UNREACHABLE, with the page sentence you relied on.
Items, one per line:
{\"ref\":\"card:question\",\"text\":\"Can SAE feature coordinates make a transformer's layer-to-layer transition linear enough to compose out of order by repeated squaring, giving parallel-in-time depth [s_pdiag]?\",\"sources\":[{\"id\":\"s_pdiag\",\"url\":\"https://parallel-in-time.org/methods/paradiag.html\",\"quote\":\"The idea is to diagonalize the time stepping matrix, keeping the matrices for the space discretization unchanged, and then to solve all time steps in parallel.\"}]}
{\"ref\":\"card:hypothesis\",\"text\":\"If residual-stream layers are near-linear in SAE features, repeated squaring composes the trajectory in O(log T) depth with no forgetting [s_pdiag].\",\"sources\":[{\"id\":\"s_pdiag\",\"url\":\"https://parallel-in-time.org/methods/paradiag.html\",\"quote\":\"The idea is to diagonalize the time stepping matrix, keeping the matrices for the space discretization unchanged, and then to solve all time steps in parallel.\"}]}
{\"ref\":\"card:if_lever\",\"text\":\"Composed out-of-order prediction reconstructs at least 90% of true activations at all 42 layers, so the linear ParaDiag shortcut holds.\",\"sources\":[]}
{\"ref\":\"card:if_not\",\"text\":\"Composed prediction falls below 90% reconstruction and decays with depth, so residual nonlinearity breaks out-of-order composition.\",\"sources\":[]}
{\"ref\":\"card:test_summary\",\"text\":\"Fit per-layer linear feature transitions (Gemma 2 9B, Gemma Scope SAEs), compose them out of order by repeated squaring, and score against the sequential forward pass; compare with the published adjacent-layer transport R² of 0.95 [s_ato].\",\"sources\":[{\"id\":\"s_ato\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2508.17540\",\"quote\":\"linear maps from upstream to downstream residuals k layers later, evaluated in feature space using downstream SAE decoder projections ... This compute-light (no finetuning, < 50 GPU-h) method ... Most linear transport occurs in nearby layers and deteriorates over large distances\"}]}
{\"ref\":\"card:closest_prior\",\"text\":\"Prior work (Activation Transport Operators) measured per-feature R² of linear layer-to-layer maps up to a 10-layer leap on a small open model, in under 50 GPU-hours [s_ato].\",\"sources\":[{\"id\":\"s_ato\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2508.17540\",\"quote\":\"linear maps from upstream to downstream residuals k layers later, evaluated in feature space using downstream SAE decoder projections ... This compute-light (no finetuning, < 50 GPU-h) method ... Most linear transport occurs in nearby layers and deteriorates over large distances\"}],\"value\":10}
{\"ref\":\"card:left_open\",\"text\":\"It did not compose operators out of order over full depth, sweep SAE width, or test time-invariance across contexts on a production-scale model.\",\"sources\":[]}
{\"ref\":\"card:anchor\",\"text\":\"SAE encoder-decoder round-trip reproduces released weights to relative error under 1e-5; composition code matches sequential on a synthetic linear system.\",\"sources\":[{\"id\":\"s_scaling_mono\",\"url\":\"https://transformer-circuits.pub/2024/scaling-monosemanticity/\",\"quote\":\"We trained three SAEs of varying sizes: 1,048,576 (~1M), 4,194,304 (~4M), and 33,554,432 (~34M) features. ... For all three SAEs, the average number of features active (i.e. with nonzero activations) on a given token was fewer than 300, and the SAE reconstruction explained at least 65% of the variance of the model activations.\"}],\"value\":0.65}
{\"ref\":\"card:limits\",\"text\":\"Holds for open Gemma/LLaMA models and released SAEs, not Claude 3 Sonnet's 34M features; it measures linearity, not a shipped parallel sampler.\",\"sources\":[]}
{\"ref\":\"src:s_pdiag\",\"url\":\"https://parallel-in-time.org/methods/paradiag.html\",\"quote\":\"The idea is to diagonalize the time stepping matrix, keeping the matrices for the space discretization unchanged, and then to solve all time steps in parallel.\",\"supports\":\"the ParaDiag/diagonalisation parallel-in-time exception the hypothesis builds on\"}
{\"ref\":\"src:s_ato\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2508.17540\",\"quote\":\"linear maps from upstream to downstream residuals k layers later, evaluated in feature space using downstream SAE decoder projections ... This compute-light (no finetuning, < 50 GPU-h) method ... Most linear transport occurs in nearby layers and deteriorates over large distances\",\"supports\":\"closest prior: per-feature R² of linear layer-to-layer maps, < 50 GPU-h, decay with distance\"}
{\"ref\":\"src:s_scaling_mono\",\"url\":\"https://transformer-circuits.pub/2024/scaling-monosemanticity/\",\"quote\":\"We trained three SAEs of varying sizes: 1,048,576 (~1M), 4,194,304 (~4M), and 33,554,432 (~34M) features. ... For all three SAEs, the average number of features active (i.e. with nonzero activations) on a given token was fewer than 300, and the SAE reconstruction explained at least 65% of the variance of the model activations.\",\"supports\":\"published SAE scale (34M features) and 65% reconstruction ceiling used as anchor context\"}
{\"ref\":\"num:layers_gemma9b\",\"name\":\"Gemma 2 9B layers\",\"value\":42,\"unit\":\"layers\",\"basis\":\"CITED\",\"quote\":\"d_model | 2304 | 3584 | 4608 ... Layers | 26 | 42 | 46 (9B column: d_model 3584, 42 layers)\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2408.00118\"}
{\"ref\":\"num:d_model_gemma9b\",\"name\":\"Gemma 2 9B model dimension\",\"value\":3584,\"unit\":\"count\",\"basis\":\"CITED\",\"quote\":\"d_model | 2304 | 3584 | 4608 (9B column)\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2408.00118\"}
{\"ref\":\"num:params_gemma9b\",\"name\":\"Gemma 2 9B parameters\",\"value\":9000000000,\"unit\":\"parameters\",\"basis\":\"CITED\",\"quote\":\"Parameters | 2B | 9B | 27B\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2408.00118\"}
{\"ref\":\"num:params_llama2\",\"name\":\"LLaMA-2 7B parameters\",\"value\":7000000000,\"unit\":\"parameters\",\"basis\":\"CITED\",\"quote\":\"we develop and release Llama 2, a collection of pretrained and fine-tuned large language models (LLMs) ranging in scale from 7 billion to 70 billion parameters.\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2307.09288\"}
{\"ref\":\"num:sae_w16\",\"name\":\"Gemma Scope 16.4K SAE width\",\"value\":16384,\"unit\":\"features\",\"basis\":\"CITED\",\"quote\":\"| 9B PT | 2^14 | All | All | All | 4B | (42 layers)\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2408.05147\"}
{\"ref\":\"num:sae_w131\",\"name\":\"Gemma Scope 131K SAE width (all layers)\",\"value\":131072,\"unit\":\"features\",\"basis\":\"CITED\",\"quote\":\"| 9B PT | ... | 2^17 | All | All | All | 8B | (42 layers)\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2408.05147\"}
{\"ref\":\"num:sae_w1m\",\"name\":\"Gemma Scope 1M SAE width (layers 9,20,31)\",\"value\":1048576,\"unit\":\"features\",\"basis\":\"CITED\",\"quote\":\"| 9B PT | ... | 2^20 | ✗ | ✗ | { 9, 20, 31} | 16B | (42 layers)\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2408.05147\"}
{\"ref\":\"num:gpu_eff_tflops\",\"name\":\"effective TFLOPS per H100 for SAE encode\",\"value\":200,\"unit\":\"TFLOPS\",\"basis\":\"PROXY\",\"quote\":\"TF32 Tensor Core | 989 TFLOPS* ... * Shown with sparsity. Specifications 1/2 lower without sparsity.\",\"page_setting\":\"NVIDIA H100 SXM datasheet peak TF32 throughput (494.5 TFLOPS dense)\",\"differs_in\":[\"real-world matmul efficiency below the dense TF32 peak for JumpReLU/SAE kernels\"],\"url\":\"https://www.cisco.com/c/dam/en/us/products/collateral/servers-unified-computing/ucs-c-series-rack-servers/nvidia-h100-80-gpu.pdf\"}

